One other option is to use different min max sizes for each volume

```
    volumes = gmsh.model.getEntities(3)

    if isinstance(min_mesh_size, (float, int)):
        gmsh.option.setNumber("Mesh.MeshSizeMin", min_mesh_size)
    else:
        # Set different mesh sizes for different volumes
        if len(min_mesh_size) == len(volumes):
            for i, volume in enumerate(volumes):
                gmsh.model.mesh.setSize(gmsh.model.getBoundary([volume]), min_mesh_size[i])
        else:
            raise ValueError(f"min_mesh_size should either be a number (float or int) or it should be an iterable with same length as the number of volumes. Not {min_mesh_size}")

    if isinstance(max_mesh_size, (float, int)):
        gmsh.option.setNumber("Mesh.MeshSizeMax", max_mesh_size)
    else:
        # Set different mesh sizes for different volumes
        if len(max_mesh_size) == len(volumes):
            for i, volume in enumerate(volumes):
                gmsh.model.mesh.setSize(gmsh.model.getBoundary([volume]), max_mesh_size[i])
        else:
            raise ValueError(f"max_mesh_size should either be a number (float or int) or it should be an iterable with same length as the number of volumes. Not {max_mesh_size}")

```

@pshriwise @makeclean @jmwright @ahnaf-tahmid-chowdhury  just wanted to show this PR. We now have a method of making dagmc h5m files without pymoab. Instead we can use native h5py (which is available on pypi)

this is passing the ci in the repo and also running particle transport on the [model-benchmar-zoo](https://github.com/fusion-energy/model_benchmark_zoo) repo and getting the same result as CSG equivalent models.

together with other ideas like [this method](https://github.com/shimwell/dagmc_volume) of getting volumes from a h5m file using just h5py I think I can see a future where I can make a few more packages pip installable as I'm no longer blocked by lack of pymoab on pip.
